<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Paris (1856)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1856">1856</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Peace of Paris (1856), Congress of Paris (1856) · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1857">Treaty of Paris</a>, signed on 30 March 1856 at the Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, ended the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimean-war">Crimean War</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> and the alliance of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>. It neutralised the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, closing it to warships, guaranteed the integrity of the Ottoman Empire, and returned southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bessarabia">Bessarabia</a> to Moldavia. Moldavia and Wallachia were placed under the collective guarantee of the great powers, ending the Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-paris-1856.jpg" alt="Treaty of Paris (1856)" width="320" height="191" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edouard Louis Dubufe · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edouard_Dubufe_Congr%C3%A8s_de_Paris.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
